Across the world, politicians use propaganda to manipulate the thoughts and actions of others. It's a strategy deployed by democracies and dictatorships alike. How, then, can I detect propaganda?

The 2026 US-Iran war was not just fought on the battlefield, but also across social media. &#160;

AI-generated animations inspired by "The Lego Movie"&#160;showcased Iran's military power, while another animated video depicted a supersized version of US Vice President JD Vance rampaging through Tehran like Godzilla. &#160;

Accounts sharing this kind of content have amassed millions of views and offer a powerful example of propaganda in the age of artificial intelligence.&#160;

They also illustrate how propaganda, while far from a new phenomenon, continues to evolve alongside technology.&#160;

DW Fact check offers tips to help you navigate today's information landscape.&#160;

Propaganda is often described as the deliberate use of information by governments, politicians and other actors&#160;to shape people’s beliefs, attitudes or behavior.&#160;

It relies on the selective presentation of information, emphasizing certain facts while omitting or misrepresenting others.&#160;

Propaganda is not only deployed by authoritarian countries like Russia, China and North Korea; it can be found all over the world.&#160;

A key difference between systems is that propaganda in authoritarian states is often heavily centralized and tightly controlled. &#160;

In democratic societies, propaganda is often more pluralistic, produced by a wider range of actors.&#160;

Moreover, freely accessing alternative media outlets and information is far more straightforward in democracies.&#160;

Talking to DW, Piers Robinson, a political scientist with the Organisation for Propaganda Studies, said propaganda can persuade someone to act or behave in a particular way. &#160;

He cited the example of how US politicians worked to convince the public to support the 2003 invasion of Iraq.&#160;

"In that case, famously, they exaggerated intelligence about weapons of mass destruction to make Iraq appear a bigger threat than it really was," said Robinson.&#160;&#160;

No weapons of mass destruction were ever found in the country. Images presented by the CIA purporting to prove their existence constituted misinformation deployed for propaganda purposes.&#160;

The Iraq War demonstrates another key aim of propaganda: vilifying political opponents, rival countries, or cultures or values seen as undesirable or threatening.&#160;

Researchers who have studied his political rhetoric point to his frequent use of name-calling as a technique for discrediting political opponents and to his use of exaggerated claims to portray undocumented immigrants disparagingly.&#160;

